      "Chapter 11 — Opportunity 3: Real estate and property operations\nThe source mentions low-level real estate development, house flipping, short-term rental arbitrage, and buying properties to rent.",
      "These are not simple passive-income methods. They involve capital, contracts, local regulation, financing, property condition, vacancy, maintenance, and market risk.",
      "House flipping\nA simplified house-flipping process:\n1. Identify a property with a plausible value-add opportunity.\n2. Research comparable sales and local demand.\n3. Inspect the property and estimate repairs.\n4. Calculate purchase, financing, legal, tax, renovation, holding, and selling costs.\n5. Stress-test the budget for delays and cost overruns.\n6. Confirm permits and legal requirements.\n7. Buy only if the risk-adjusted economics make sense.\n8. Manage the renovation.\n9. Sell or refinance according to the original plan.\nA common mistake is to calculate profit as resale price minus purchase price. That ignores most of the costs.",
      "Estimated project profit = sale proceeds − purchase price − transaction costs − financing costs − renovation costs − holding costs − taxes − selling costs − contingency.",
      "Short-term rental arbitrage\nArbitrage usually means leasing a property and then operating it as a short-term rental, where the lease, building rules, local laws, and platform terms permit it.",
      "Before entering:\n- Obtain explicit written permission from the owner.\n- Verify local short-term rental rules.\n- Check building or association restrictions.\n- Calculate rent, utilities, cleaning, furnishing, insurance, taxes, platform fees, and maintenance.\n- Model occupancy and nightly rates conservatively.\n- Plan for seasonality and vacancies.\n- Understand who is liable for guest damage and complaints.\nDo not assume you can legally sublet or operate a short-term rental simply because a platform allows listings.",
