    "id": "chapter-14",
    "number": 14,
    "title": "Chapter 14 — Scale What Already Works",
    "paragraphs": [
      "Chapter 14 — Scale what already works\nThe source describes Alpha Chad as having $100,000 or more saved, alongside a business or high-paying job. Its advice is to do many of the same things as Level 3, but at a larger scale.",
      "The key point is that larger capital can make larger opportunities possible. But larger capital also means larger losses if judgement is poor.",
      "The source gives examples such as moving from flipping a $300,000 house to a $1 million house. The underlying principle is not that a more expensive property is automatically better. It is that a proven skill or business process may be applied to a larger opportunity when the risk, financing, and expected return justify it.",
      "Scale the process, not just the transaction size\nBefore increasing the amount of capital:\n1. Prove the smaller version works.\n2. Understand what drove the result.\n3. Identify the constraints.\n4. Stress-test the larger version.\n5. Confirm that the team and systems can handle it.\n6. Maintain adequate liquidity.\n7. Define a maximum acceptable loss.\n8. Increase exposure gradually.\nA larger deal magnifies both good execution and mistakes."
    ]
